/* Sec+ Gym Cards — part d34 */
export const CARDS_D34 = [
 /* ---------- vulns (16) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Memory injection — what class of vulnerability is it?",a:"#Attacker code placed into a running process's memory\nDLL injection and shellcode in a hijacked thread are classic forms",s:"Code inserted into a live process's memory space",
  x:"Memory injection covers any technique that writes attacker-controlled code or data into the address space of a running process and then gets it executed, such as DLL injection or process hollowing. It matters because the malicious code inherits the trust and privileges of the host process, which is why EDR tools watch for cross-process memory writes. The exam phrases it as 'code injected into a legitimate process' or 'DLL injection'; contrast with buffer overflow, which is a specific way to corrupt memory rather than the general act of injecting into it."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Buffer overflow — the core mechanism",a:"#Input exceeds a fixed-size buffer and overwrites adjacent memory\nCan clobber the return address so execution jumps to attacker code",s:"Oversized input overwrites neighboring memory",
  x:"A buffer overflow happens when a program copies more data into a buffer than it was allocated for, so the excess spills into adjacent memory such as the stack return pointer. By carefully crafting the input, an attacker redirects execution to shellcode they supplied. On the exam, 'more data than the buffer can hold', 'stack' or 'heap' overflow, and mitigations like ASLR, DEP/NX, and bounds checking all point here. Input validation and memory-safe languages are the root fixes.",
  w:"Overflow = the cup runs over onto the table next to it."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Race condition / TOCTOU — what is the flaw?",a:"#Outcome depends on timing between check and use\nA resource changes between when it is verified and when it is acted on",s:"Timing gap between a check and its later use",
  x:"A race condition exists when the correct result depends on the precise ordering of concurrent operations. The classic security case is time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU): a program validates a file or permission, then a second thread or attacker swaps the resource before the program uses it. Exam signals are 'timing', 'concurrent', 'between the check and the use', or a symbolic-link swap. The fix is atomic operations or locking so check and use cannot be separated.",
  w:"TOCTOU: the gap between looking and leaping."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Malicious update — the vulnerability",a:"#Trusted update channel delivers attacker code\nSigned or auto-installed updates spread compromise widely",s:"Compromised update pushed through a trusted channel",
  x:"A malicious update vulnerability arises when the mechanism organizations trust to deliver patches is itself abused, either by hijacking the vendor's build pipeline or by tricking clients into pulling a fake update. Because updates run with high privilege on many hosts at once, one poisoned release can compromise thousands of victims, as SolarWinds showed. Exam wording like 'software update contained a backdoor' or 'vendor build server compromised' signals this; verifying code signatures and using controlled update servers are the defenses."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"OS-based vulnerability — what does the term mean?",a:"#Flaw in the operating system or its services\nKernel, drivers, and built-in services exploited for privilege escalation",s:"Weakness in the OS kernel, drivers, or services",
  x:"OS-based vulnerabilities are defects in the operating system itself, including the kernel, device drivers, and bundled services, rather than in third-party applications. They are especially dangerous because exploiting them often yields SYSTEM or root, enabling privilege escalation and persistence. On the exam, references to a missing OS patch, an unsupported version, or a kernel flaw point to this class, and the remedy is timely patching, hardening, and retiring end-of-life releases."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Web-based vulnerabilities — SQL injection vs cross-site scripting",a:"#SQLi targets the database; XSS targets other users' browsers\nBoth stem from untrusted input not being validated or encoded",s:"SQLi hits the database, XSS runs in a victim's browser",
  x:"SQL injection inserts database commands through an unvalidated input field so the server's query does something the developer never intended, such as dumping every row. Cross-site scripting instead injects script that is stored or reflected by the site and then executed in another user's browser, stealing sessions or defacing content. On the exam, quotes and OR 1=1 mean SQLi; script tags or 'runs in the victim's browser' mean XSS. Both are prevented by input validation, parameterized queries, and output encoding."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Firmware and end-of-life hardware — why are they a vulnerability?",a:"#No more patches once vendor support ends\nLegacy and EOL devices keep known flaws forever",s:"Unsupported devices never get fixes for known flaws",
  x:"Hardware vulnerabilities include exploitable bugs in firmware such as BIOS/UEFI or device controllers, and the structural problem of end-of-life or legacy equipment that the vendor no longer patches. Once support ends every newly discovered flaw stays open, so the device becomes a permanent foothold. On the exam, 'the vendor no longer supports', 'legacy', or 'firmware' points to this class, and the answers are compensating controls such as isolation and segmentation, or decommissioning the device."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"VM escape",a:"#Break out of a guest to attack the hypervisor or other VMs\nDefeats the isolation virtualization is supposed to guarantee",s:"Guest breaks out to the hypervisor or neighboring VMs",
  x:"VM escape is an exploit that lets code running inside a virtual machine interact directly with the hypervisor or host, and from there with other guests on the same hardware. It is severe because tenants in shared virtual environments rely entirely on hypervisor isolation. On the exam, 'attacker in one VM accesses another VM on the same host' means VM escape, distinct from VM sprawl, which is simply unmanaged VM growth. Patching the hypervisor and limiting guest-to-host integrations are the controls."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Resource reuse in virtualization/cloud",a:"#Freed memory or storage handed to a new tenant still holds old data\nSensitive remnants leak across workloads",s:"Recycled memory or disk exposes a previous tenant's data",
  x:"Resource reuse is a virtualization and cloud vulnerability where RAM, disk blocks, or other resources released by one workload are allocated to another without being wiped, so the new tenant can read leftover secrets. It is the multi-tenant version of a data remnant problem. Exam wording such as 'data from a previous virtual machine was found' or 'deallocated memory' signals it, and the mitigation is for the hypervisor or provider to zero resources before reassignment."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Cloud-specific vulnerabilities — the recurring examples",a:"#Misconfigured storage, weak API keys, and shared-tenancy exposure\nThe customer's side of the responsibility matrix is where they appear",s:"Open buckets, leaked API keys, and multi-tenant exposure",
  x:"Cloud-specific vulnerabilities cluster around things the customer controls in a shared platform: publicly readable storage buckets, over-privileged identity roles, API keys committed to code, and insecure interfaces, plus platform-level risks from sharing hardware with other tenants. The exam frames these as 'storage bucket exposed to the internet' or 'credentials hardcoded in a cloud deployment'. Remember the responsibility matrix: the provider secures the underlying cloud, but configuration of what you deploy is on you."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Supply chain vulnerability — the three provider types",a:"#Service provider, hardware provider, software provider\nA compromise at any link is inherited by every downstream customer",s:"Risk inherited from service, hardware, or software suppliers",
  x:"SY0-701 breaks supply chain vulnerabilities into three sources: service providers such as MSPs with remote access into your environment, hardware providers whose devices may ship with implanted or counterfeit components, and software providers whose libraries or builds may be trojanized. The point is that your security depends on parties you do not control. On the exam, 'a vendor was breached and the attacker used that access' or 'counterfeit network gear' signals supply chain, and SBOMs, vendor assessment, and verified sourcing are the controls."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Cryptographic vulnerabilities — what usually goes wrong?",a:"#Weak or deprecated algorithms, short keys, and poor key management\nStrong math is undone by bad implementation or sloppy handling",s:"Deprecated ciphers, short keys, or mishandled keys",
  x:"Cryptographic vulnerabilities rarely mean the math was broken; they mean an obsolete algorithm like DES or MD5 was chosen, key lengths were too short, random numbers were predictable, or private keys were stored or transmitted insecurely. Downgrade attacks that force a weaker cipher suite belong here too. On the exam, 'uses SSL 3.0', 'MD5 hashes', or 'private key stored in a public repository' points to a cryptographic weakness; the fix is current algorithms, adequate key lengths, and proper key lifecycle management."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Misconfiguration as a vulnerability",a:"#Insecure settings rather than a software defect\nDefault passwords, open ports, and overly broad permissions",s:"Insecure settings such as defaults and open services",
  x:"Misconfiguration is any deviation from a secure setup that a system's own controls could have prevented: default credentials left in place, unnecessary services running, permissive firewall rules, verbose error messages, or wildcard permissions. Unlike a code bug it needs no patch, only correct settings, which is why secure baselines and configuration enforcement exist. On the exam, 'default account still enabled' or 'unneeded service listening' signals misconfiguration rather than a zero-day or software flaw."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Mobile device vulnerabilities — side-loading vs jailbreaking",a:"#Side-loading installs apps outside the official store; jailbreaking removes OS restrictions\nBoth bypass the platform's vetting and sandbox",s:"Unofficial app installs vs removing OS root restrictions",
  x:"Side-loading means installing an app from a source other than the vendor's curated store, skipping the malware screening that store submission requires. Jailbreaking (iOS) or rooting (Android) removes the operating system's privilege restrictions entirely so any app can gain full control. Both defeat the sandbox model that mobile security depends on. The exam expects MDM policies that block side-loading and detect rooted or jailbroken devices; 'app from an untrusted source' is side-loading, 'removed manufacturer restrictions' is jailbreaking."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"vulns",q:"Zero-day vulnerability",a:"#Flaw exploited before the vendor has a patch\nSignature-based tools have nothing to match",s:"Exploited before any vendor fix exists",
  x:"A zero-day is a vulnerability unknown to the vendor or unpatched at the time it is being exploited, so defenders have had zero days to prepare. Signature-based antivirus and IPS cannot catch it because there is no known pattern yet; behavior-based detection, segmentation, and least privilege limit the damage instead. On the exam, 'no patch is available' or 'previously unknown vulnerability' means zero-day, and the best answer usually favors compensating controls and anomaly detection over 'apply the patch'."},
 {obj:"2.5",c:"vulns",q:"Mitigation techniques — segmentation, allow lists, and least privilege",a:"#Shrink what a compromise can reach\nIsolate systems, permit only known-good software, and grant minimal rights",s:"Contain via isolation, allow lists, and least privilege",
  x:"SY0-701 lists general mitigations: segmentation and isolation to contain lateral movement, ACLs and application allow lists to permit only approved traffic and software, patching and encryption, monitoring, least privilege, configuration enforcement to keep hosts on baseline, and decommissioning what cannot be secured. Hardening adds removing unneeded software, closing unused ports, and changing defaults. The exam usually asks which mitigation fits when patching is impossible; the answer is a compensating one like segmentation or an allow list."},

 /* ---------- arch (20) ---------- */
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Cloud responsibility matrix",a:"#Who secures what, split between provider and customer\nThe provider covers the cloud itself; the customer covers what they put in it",s:"Chart of provider vs customer security duties",
  x:"The responsibility matrix (shared responsibility model) documents which security tasks belong to the cloud service provider and which remain with the customer, and the split moves depending on the service model. The provider always owns physical facilities and the hypervisor; the customer always owns their data, identities, and access decisions. The exam tests it with questions like 'in IaaS, who patches the guest OS' (customer) or 'who secures the physical data center' (provider). Misreading the matrix is a leading cause of cloud misconfiguration."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Hybrid cloud considerations",a:"#On-prem and cloud combined into one environment\nConsistency of controls across both halves is the hard part",s:"On-premises plus cloud operated as one environment",
  x:"Hybrid describes an architecture that mixes on-premises infrastructure with public or private cloud services, often with data or workloads moving between them. The security challenge is keeping identity, encryption, logging, and network policy consistent on both sides, because attackers exploit the seams. On the exam, 'extend the data center into the cloud' or 'burst to the cloud during peak demand' signals hybrid, and answers favor unified identity (federation), consistent baselines, and secure interconnects such as VPN or dedicated links."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"IaaS — what the customer manages",a:"#Provider supplies compute, storage, and network; customer runs everything above\nCustomer patches the OS, apps, and data",s:"Rent VMs and storage, you manage OS upward",g:"cloudmodel",
  x:"Infrastructure as a Service delivers raw building blocks, typically virtual machines, block storage, and virtual networking, on which the customer installs and maintains the operating system, middleware, applications, and data. It gives the most control and the most responsibility of the three models. On the exam, 'the organization needs to manage the operating system' or 'provision virtual servers' points to IaaS; in the responsibility matrix the customer patches the guest OS while the provider secures the hypervisor and hardware.",
  w:"IaaS: they hand you an empty server rack in the cloud."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"PaaS — what the customer manages",a:"#Provider runs the OS and runtime; customer deploys code and data\nDevelopers build without maintaining servers",s:"Managed runtime, you supply application code and data",g:"cloudmodel",
  x:"Platform as a Service provides a managed environment for running applications, including the operating system, runtime, and often databases, so developers push code and configuration without patching servers. The customer is responsible for the application logic, its data, and access to it. Exam cues include 'developers want to deploy applications without managing the underlying servers' or 'a managed database service'; PaaS sits between IaaS and SaaS in the responsibility matrix.",
  w:"PaaS: they hand you the workbench, you bring the project."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"SaaS — what the customer manages",a:"#Provider runs the whole application; customer manages users and data\nLeast control, least operational burden",s:"Complete hosted application, you handle users and data",g:"cloudmodel",
  x:"Software as a Service delivers a finished application over the network, such as hosted email or CRM, with the provider handling infrastructure, platform, and application code. The customer's remaining duties are configuring the service securely, managing identities and permissions, and protecting the data placed in it. On the exam, 'subscription to a web-based application' or 'the vendor maintains all updates' signals SaaS, and the customer still owns access control and data classification decisions.",
  w:"SaaS: they hand you the finished product, just log in."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Public vs private cloud deployment",a:"#Public is shared multi-tenant; private is dedicated to one organization\nPrivate trades cost for control and isolation",s:"Shared multi-tenant vs single-organization dedicated",
  x:"In a public cloud the provider's infrastructure is shared among many customers, delivering low cost and elastic scale but relying on logical separation between tenants. A private cloud is built for and used by a single organization, either on its own premises or hosted by a third party, giving stronger isolation and regulatory control at higher cost. The exam pairs 'multi-tenant' with public and 'dedicated to one organization' or 'strict compliance requirement' with private."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Community cloud",a:"#Shared by organizations with common needs\nSeveral agencies or firms in one sector pool a cloud",s:"Cloud shared among organizations with a common mission",
  x:"A community cloud is infrastructure shared by several organizations that have similar requirements, such as government agencies subject to the same regulations or hospitals under the same privacy rules. Costs and governance are shared among the members rather than borne by one owner or opened to the general public. On the exam, 'multiple organizations with shared compliance concerns' or 'a consortium of universities' signals community rather than public, private, or hybrid."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Infrastructure as Code (IaC)",a:"#Environments defined and deployed from versioned templates\nRepeatable builds, but a flawed template replicates flaws everywhere",s:"Provision infrastructure from version-controlled templates",
  x:"Infrastructure as Code describes servers, networks, and cloud resources in machine-readable definition files (Terraform, CloudFormation, Ansible) that are stored in version control and applied automatically. It eliminates configuration drift and makes rebuilds reproducible, which supports resilience and rapid recovery. The security caveat is that a mistake or secret in the template is deployed at scale, so templates need review and scanning. On the exam, 'deploy identical environments repeatedly' or 'template' points to IaC."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Serverless architecture",a:"#Code runs as event-triggered functions; the provider manages all servers\nYou secure the function code and its permissions",s:"Event-driven functions with no server management",
  x:"Serverless (functions as a service) lets developers upload small units of code that run only when triggered by an event and are billed per execution, while the provider handles provisioning, scaling, and patching of the underlying hosts. Security focus shifts to the code itself, its IAM permissions, and the APIs it calls, because there is no OS to harden. On the exam, 'pay only when the function executes' or 'no infrastructure to maintain' means serverless, and a common pitfall is over-privileged function roles."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Microservices architecture",a:"#Application split into small, independently deployed services\nMany APIs to secure, but failures are contained",s:"App built from small independently deployable services",
  x:"A microservices design breaks a monolithic application into loosely coupled services that each own one capability and communicate over APIs, allowing independent scaling, deployment, and technology choices. Security implications are the growth of the attack surface across many service-to-service calls, the need for mutual authentication and API gateways, and the benefit that a compromised service is limited in scope. The exam cues 'loosely coupled', 'API-based', or 'independently updated components'."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Software-defined networking (SDN)",a:"#Control plane separated from the data plane\nA central controller programs switches through APIs",s:"Central controller programs forwarding via APIs",
  x:"SDN abstracts network control into a software controller that pushes forwarding rules to switches and routers, separating the decision-making control plane from the packet-forwarding data plane. Applications talk to the controller through a northbound API; the controller talks to devices through a southbound API. It enables rapid, policy-driven segmentation and automated response but makes the controller a high-value target. On the exam, 'programmable network' or 'centralized network control' means SDN."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Air-gapped vs logical segmentation",a:"#Air gap is a physical disconnect; logical uses VLANs, firewalls, or SDN\nAir gap is stronger but harder to operate",s:"Physical disconnection vs VLAN/firewall separation",
  x:"An air-gapped system has no network connection to other systems at all, so data must move by removable media, which makes it the strongest isolation for classified or safety-critical environments but also the least convenient. Logical segmentation keeps devices on shared physical infrastructure while separating them with VLANs, firewall zones, or software-defined policies. The exam treats 'no network connection' as air gap and 'VLAN' or 'separate zone' as logical segmentation; USB media is the classic air-gap breach."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Centralized vs decentralized architecture",a:"#Centralized concentrates control and risk; decentralized spreads both\nOne easy-to-manage target versus many harder-to-govern ones",s:"Single point of control vs distributed control",
  x:"A centralized architecture places control, processing, or data in one place, making it easier to secure, monitor, and update consistently but creating a single point of failure and an attractive target. A decentralized design distributes those functions across many nodes or sites, improving resilience and local autonomy at the cost of consistent policy enforcement and visibility. The exam frames the trade-off as 'ease of management and single point of failure' versus 'resilience and harder governance'."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Containerization vs virtualization",a:"#Containers share the host kernel; VMs each run a full OS\nContainers are lighter but isolation is weaker",s:"Shared-kernel containers vs full-OS virtual machines",
  x:"Virtualization runs complete guest operating systems on a hypervisor, giving strong isolation at the cost of memory and startup time. Containerization packages an application with its libraries and runs it on the host's kernel via a container engine such as Docker, so containers start in seconds and are far denser, but a kernel exploit affects every container on the host. On the exam, 'shares the host operating system kernel' means container; 'each instance has its own operating system' means VM."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"IoT security concerns",a:"#Cheap, always-on devices with weak defaults and rare patches\nOften need isolation on their own network segment",s:"Weak defaults and no patching on connected devices",
  x:"Internet of Things devices such as cameras, sensors, and smart appliances typically ship with default credentials, minimal processing power for security features, and little or no vendor update support, yet they sit on the network permanently. Botnets like Mirai were built from exactly this. The exam expects mitigations such as changing defaults, placing IoT on a segregated VLAN, and disabling unneeded services; 'smart devices' or 'sensors that cannot run an agent' signals IoT."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"ICS / SCADA systems",a:"#Control physical processes; availability and safety come first\nLegacy protocols with no authentication, rarely patched",s:"Industrial control systems prioritizing uptime and safety",
  x:"Industrial control systems and their supervisory SCADA layer run power plants, water treatment, and manufacturing lines using PLCs and protocols like Modbus that were designed for reliability, not security. Because downtime can be physically dangerous, patching windows are rare, so isolation from the corporate network, one-way data diodes, and strict change control are the primary protections. On the exam, 'operational technology', 'PLC', or 'utility control network' points to ICS/SCADA, and availability outranks confidentiality."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Real-time operating system (RTOS)",a:"#OS that guarantees response within a fixed time\nPredictability matters more than features or security add-ons",s:"OS guaranteeing deterministic timing for control tasks",
  x:"An RTOS is a minimal operating system built to process inputs within strict, predictable deadlines, which is essential for devices like medical equipment, vehicle controllers, and industrial sensors. Its small footprint leaves little room for security agents or patch mechanisms, and vendors often lock the firmware. On the exam, 'deterministic', 'must respond within milliseconds', or an embedded controller signals RTOS, and the mitigation is usually network isolation and vendor firmware management rather than host-based tools."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Embedded systems — the security constraint",a:"#Purpose-built hardware and firmware with limited compute\nCannot run agents, hard to patch, long service life",s:"Fixed-function devices with little room for security tooling",
  x:"Embedded systems are computers built into a larger device to perform a dedicated function, from medical pumps to vehicle ECUs and printers. Their constraints are low compute and power budgets, proprietary firmware that may never be updated, and lifespans measured in decades. The exam groups them with IoT, RTOS, and SCADA and expects you to choose compensating controls: segmentation, monitoring, and restricting connectivity. 'Cannot install an agent' or 'firmware-based device' points to embedded."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"High availability as an architecture goal",a:"#Eliminate single points of failure so service survives component loss\nRedundant power, links, nodes, and sites",s:"Redundancy so service continues through component failure",
  x:"High availability designs systems so that failure of any single component, whether a disk, a server, a link, or a whole site, does not interrupt the service, usually by clustering, load balancing, and redundant paths. It is measured in uptime percentages such as 'five nines'. On the exam, 'no single point of failure' or 'must remain available during hardware failure' signals HA, and it belongs with resilience considerations alongside cost, scalability, and complexity."},
 {obj:"3.1",c:"arch",q:"Architecture considerations — patch availability and inability to patch",a:"#Choose designs whose components can actually be updated\nWhen patching is impossible, isolate and compensate",s:"Weigh whether components can be updated before adopting them",
  x:"SY0-701 lists design considerations including availability, resilience, cost, scalability, ease of deployment and recovery, risk transference, patch availability, inability to patch, power, and compute. Patch availability asks whether the vendor actively publishes fixes; inability to patch covers devices like ICS and embedded gear that cannot be taken down or altered. The exam expects such systems to get segmentation, monitoring, and compensating controls, and notes that power and compute limits (as in IoT) constrain which tools you can run."},

 /* ---------- netsec (26) ---------- */
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Device placement and security zones — the design principle",a:"#Group systems by trust level and put controls at the boundaries\nInternet, screened subnet, internal, and management zones",s:"Segment by trust level with controls at each boundary",
  x:"Secure network design starts by defining zones of differing trust, such as the untrusted internet, a semi-trusted screened subnet for public-facing servers, the trusted internal LAN, and a tightly restricted management zone, then placing firewalls, sensors, and proxies at the borders between them. Where a device sits determines what it can see and what it can protect. The exam asks where to place a sensor, a jump server, or a web server; the answer follows the zone model.",
  v:"dmz"},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Screened subnet",a:"#Isolated segment for public-facing servers between two firewall boundaries\nInternet can reach it; it cannot freely reach the internal LAN",s:"Buffer segment for public servers, walled off from the LAN",v:"dmz",
  x:"A screened subnet, formerly called a DMZ, is a network segment that hosts services the internet must reach, such as web, mail, and VPN gateways, with a firewall on both sides so a compromised public server still cannot initiate connections into the private network. SY0-701 uses the term screened subnet, so expect it in answer choices. 'Place the web server so that a breach does not expose internal systems' is the exam cue.",
  w:"Screened = the porch: visitors get that far and no further."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Attack surface",a:"#Sum of all points where an attacker could try to enter\nShrink it by removing services, ports, accounts, and exposures",s:"Total set of reachable entry points an attacker could use",
  x:"The attack surface is every interface, open port, service, account, API, and physical access point that could be used to compromise a system or organization. Reducing it is a foundational goal of hardening and network design: fewer listening services, fewer exposed hosts, and fewer privileged accounts mean fewer opportunities. On the exam, 'minimize the attack surface' pairs with disabling unnecessary services, closing ports, and limiting internet-facing systems."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Fail-open vs fail-closed",a:"#Fail-open keeps traffic flowing on failure; fail-closed blocks it\nAvailability versus security when a control dies",s:"Failure allows traffic vs failure blocks traffic",
  x:"Every security device must be configured for what happens when it fails. Fail-open lets traffic pass unfiltered so the business keeps running at the cost of protection; fail-closed stops all traffic so nothing unscreened gets through at the cost of an outage. High-security environments and inline IPS in sensitive zones favor fail-closed; a door that must unlock in a fire is a physical fail-open (fail-safe). The exam asks which behavior fits when security or availability is prioritized."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Active vs passive security device",a:"#Active can block or alter traffic; passive only observes and alerts\nAn IPS is active; a monitoring IDS is passive",s:"Blocks or modifies traffic vs only watches and alerts",
  x:"An active device sits in the traffic path with the ability to drop packets, reset connections, or rewrite content, whereas a passive device receives a copy of traffic and can only log, analyze, and raise alerts. Active devices provide prevention but add latency and become potential points of failure; passive devices are invisible to attackers and never disrupt service. On the exam, 'must be able to stop the attack' means active, and 'must not affect network performance' means passive."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Inline vs tap/monitor placement",a:"#Inline is in the traffic path; tap or SPAN copies traffic to the sensor\nInline enables blocking; a tap enables silent monitoring",s:"In the data path vs receiving a mirrored copy",
  x:"An inline sensor is physically or logically between the source and destination so every packet passes through it, which is required for an IPS to block but means a device failure can interrupt traffic. A tap is a hardware splitter and a SPAN or mirror port is a switch feature; both send a copy of traffic to a monitoring device that cannot affect the flow. The exam pairs inline with prevention and fail-open/closed decisions, and tap/SPAN with IDS, packet capture, and 'without introducing a point of failure'."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Jump server",a:"#Hardened host that is the only path into a secure segment\nAdmins log into it first, then onward to managed systems",s:"Single hardened hop for administrative access",
  x:"A jump server (jump box or bastion host) is a tightly locked-down system placed at the boundary of a sensitive zone; administrators authenticate to it, usually with MFA and full session logging, and only from there can they reach servers inside. This concentrates auditing and reduces the attack surface because internal management ports are closed to everything else. On the exam, 'administrators must connect through a single hardened system to reach the secure network' points to a jump server.",
  w:"Jump server: one guarded bridge into the castle."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Forward proxy vs reverse proxy",a:"#Forward proxy fronts internal clients going out; reverse proxy fronts servers receiving inbound\nForward filters users, reverse protects and balances servers",s:"Client-side outbound gateway vs server-side inbound front",
  x:"A forward proxy sits between internal users and the internet, applying content filtering, caching, and logging to outbound requests, and hiding client addresses. A reverse proxy sits in front of web servers, accepting inbound connections on their behalf so it can terminate TLS, load balance, cache, and shield the servers from direct exposure. The exam cue for forward is 'control employee web browsing'; for reverse it is 'protect web servers from direct internet access' or 'offload TLS'."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"IDS vs IPS",a:"#IDS detects and alerts; IPS detects and blocks\nIPS must be inline; IDS can work from a tap",s:"Alert-only detection vs inline blocking",
  x:"An intrusion detection system analyzes traffic or host activity against signatures and behavior baselines and generates alerts, but relies on a human or another system to respond. An intrusion prevention system does the same analysis inline and can drop packets or reset sessions automatically. IPS gives faster containment but a false positive blocks legitimate traffic, so tuning matters. On the exam, 'notify the administrator' means IDS; 'automatically stop the attack' means IPS."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Load balancer",a:"#Distributes requests across a pool of servers\nImproves availability and absorbs traffic spikes",s:"Spreads client requests across multiple servers",
  x:"A load balancer receives client connections and forwards each to one of several back-end servers using a scheduling algorithm such as round robin or least connections, often with health checks that remove failed nodes and session persistence so a user stays on one server. It provides high availability and scalability and can help mitigate volumetric DDoS. On the exam, 'stay available if one server fails' or 'distribute traffic evenly' means load balancer; active/active versus active/passive describes how nodes share work."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Sensors and collectors — what is the difference?",a:"#Sensors capture traffic or events at a point; collectors aggregate them centrally\nSensor feeds the collector, collector feeds the SIEM",s:"Point-of-capture devices vs central aggregation points",
  x:"A sensor is an agent or appliance placed on a segment or host to capture packets, flows, or log events; a collector gathers the output from many sensors and forwards or stores it, typically for a SIEM. Sensor placement decides what you can see (inside the firewall, in the screened subnet, on the management VLAN), and collectors normalize and centralize it. On the exam, 'where should the sensor be placed to see traffic between X and Y' tests placement, and 'aggregate logs from multiple sources' points to a collector."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"802.1X port-based network access control",a:"#Supplicant, authenticator, authentication server\nDevice must authenticate before the switch port or Wi-Fi passes traffic",s:"Authenticate the device before the port is opened",v:"dot1x",
  x:"IEEE 802.1X blocks all traffic on a switch port or wireless association until the connecting device (the supplicant) authenticates through the switch or access point (the authenticator) to a RADIUS server (the authentication server). It uses EAP to carry the credential exchange, and after success the port is opened and often assigned a VLAN. On the exam, 'prevent unauthorized devices from connecting to the wired network' or the trio supplicant/authenticator/authentication server points to 802.1X.",
  w:"802.1X: the bouncer at the port checks ID with the back office."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"EAP — its role in 802.1X",a:"#Framework carrying authentication methods between supplicant and server\nEAP-TLS uses certificates; PEAP wraps EAP in a TLS tunnel",s:"Extensible framework for the credential exchange",v:"dot1x",
  x:"The Extensible Authentication Protocol is not a single method but a container that lets different authentication types travel over 802.1X: EAP-TLS requires client and server certificates and is the strongest, PEAP and EAP-TTLS build a server-authenticated TLS tunnel and then send inner credentials such as MS-CHAPv2, and EAP-FAST is Cisco's variant. The exam asks which EAP type needs certificates on every client (EAP-TLS) or which protects passwords in a tunnel (PEAP)."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Web application firewall (WAF)",a:"#Inspects HTTP traffic for application-layer attacks\nBlocks SQLi, XSS, and other OWASP Top 10 patterns",s:"Layer 7 filter for HTTP attacks such as SQLi and XSS",
  x:"A WAF sits in front of web servers, often as a reverse proxy or cloud service, and parses HTTP requests and responses to detect and block injection, cross-site scripting, path traversal, and similar attacks that a network firewall cannot see. It can also virtually patch a known vulnerability until the code is fixed. On the exam, 'protect the web application from SQL injection' or 'block attacks against a specific website' means WAF rather than NGFW or IPS."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Unified threat management (UTM)",a:"#Single appliance bundling firewall, IPS, antivirus, filtering, and VPN\nSimpler for small sites, but one box is a bottleneck and single point of failure",s:"All-in-one security appliance for smaller networks",
  x:"UTM consolidates many security functions, such as stateful firewall, intrusion prevention, anti-malware, URL filtering, spam filtering, and VPN termination, into one device with one management console. It suits branch offices and small businesses that lack staff for separate tools, but performance drops as features are enabled and its failure removes every control at once. The exam contrasts UTM (many functions, simpler) with NGFW (deeper application awareness, enterprise scale)."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Next-generation firewall (NGFW)",a:"#Application-aware firewall with integrated IPS and identity context\nRules by application and user, not only port and IP",s:"Application- and user-aware firewall with built-in IPS",
  x:"A next-generation firewall extends stateful inspection with deep packet inspection that identifies applications regardless of port, integrates intrusion prevention and threat intelligence, and can write rules based on user or group identity from a directory. It can also decrypt TLS for inspection. On the exam, 'block a specific application even when it uses port 443' or 'apply policy based on user identity' signals NGFW; a plain layer 4 firewall could not do either."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Layer 4 vs layer 7 firewall",a:"#Layer 4 filters on IP, port, and connection state; layer 7 inspects application content\nLayer 7 sees the payload, layer 4 sees only the envelope",s:"Port/state filtering vs application-content inspection",
  x:"A layer 4 (stateful) firewall makes decisions from source and destination addresses, ports, and whether a packet belongs to an established session, which is fast but blind to what is inside the connection. A layer 7 firewall understands protocols like HTTP and DNS and can allow or deny based on URLs, commands, or application signatures. The exam cue for layer 7 is 'inspect the contents of the traffic' or 'identify the application'; for layer 4 it is 'based on port and protocol'."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"VPN types — remote access vs site-to-site",a:"#Remote access connects one user; site-to-site joins two networks\nClient software versus gateway-to-gateway tunnel",s:"Single-client tunnel vs permanent network-to-network tunnel",v:"vpn",
  x:"A remote access VPN lets an individual device establish an encrypted tunnel to a corporate gateway over the internet, usually with a client and user authentication. A site-to-site VPN is a persistent tunnel between two gateways, such as headquarters and a branch, so every host behind each end communicates transparently. On the exam, 'employees working from home' means remote access, while 'connect the branch office network to the main office' means site-to-site, typically using IPsec."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"Split tunnel vs full tunnel VPN",a:"#Split sends only corporate traffic through the VPN; full sends everything\nFull tunnel gives inspection, split saves bandwidth",s:"Corporate-only routing vs all traffic through the tunnel",v:"vpn",
  x:"With a full tunnel every packet from the remote client, including ordinary web browsing, is routed through the corporate VPN, so it can be filtered and monitored but consumes gateway bandwidth. A split tunnel routes only traffic destined for internal resources over the VPN and lets internet traffic go directly, which is efficient but bypasses corporate security controls and risks bridging the two networks. The exam favors full tunnel for 'ensure all remote traffic is inspected' and split tunnel for 'reduce VPN bandwidth usage'."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"TLS VPN vs IPsec VPN",a:"#TLS runs over TCP 443 and works through most firewalls; IPsec works at layer 3\nTLS suits clientless remote access, IPsec suits site-to-site",s:"Port 443 browser-friendly tunnel vs layer 3 tunnel",v:"vpn",
  x:"A TLS (SSL) VPN encapsulates traffic in a TLS session, so it passes through firewalls and NAT easily and can be delivered through a browser portal without a dedicated client, making it popular for remote users. IPsec operates at the network layer, encrypting IP packets regardless of application, and is the standard for permanent site-to-site tunnels and full network access. On the exam, 'clientless' or 'from any location including restrictive networks' points to TLS; 'connect two networks' points to IPsec."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"IPsec AH vs ESP",a:"#AH authenticates and integrity-protects but does not encrypt; ESP encrypts and can authenticate\nESP is what provides confidentiality",s:"Integrity-only header vs encrypting payload protocol",v:"vpn",
  x:"The Authentication Header protocol adds an integrity check and origin authentication covering the packet, including parts of the IP header, but leaves the data readable, and it breaks under NAT because the header changes. Encapsulating Security Payload encrypts the payload and optionally provides integrity and authentication, so it is the protocol used in virtually all deployments. On the exam, 'IPsec without encryption' means AH, and 'confidentiality' means ESP; IKE handles the key negotiation for both.",
  w:"AH = Authenticate Honestly, no secrets kept. ESP = Encrypt Something Private."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"IPsec transport mode vs tunnel mode",a:"#Transport protects only the payload; tunnel wraps the whole original packet in a new one\nTunnel mode is used gateway-to-gateway",s:"Payload-only protection vs entire packet encapsulated",v:"vpn",
  x:"In transport mode IPsec inserts its header after the original IP header and protects only the data, so the original source and destination addresses stay visible; it fits host-to-host connections. In tunnel mode the entire original packet is encrypted and placed inside a new IP packet addressed between the VPN gateways, hiding the internal addressing, which is why site-to-site VPNs use it. The exam signals tunnel mode with 'between two security gateways' and transport mode with 'end-to-end between two hosts'."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"SD-WAN",a:"#Software-managed WAN that steers traffic across multiple links by policy\nBranches use internet, MPLS, or LTE with central control and encryption",s:"Policy-driven WAN across mixed links with central control",
  x:"Software-defined wide area networking abstracts branch connectivity so a central controller decides, per application and policy, whether traffic goes over MPLS, broadband, or cellular, while building encrypted overlays between sites. It replaces rigid hub-and-spoke WANs and lets branches reach cloud services directly. On the exam, 'connect many branch offices cost-effectively over commodity internet with centralized policy' points to SD-WAN, and it is frequently the network foundation under a SASE deployment."},
 {obj:"3.2",c:"netsec",q:"SASE (Secure Access Service Edge)",a:"#Cloud-delivered bundle of SD-WAN plus security services\nFirewall, secure web gateway, CASB, and zero trust access at the edge",s:"Cloud-delivered network plus security stack at the edge",
  x:"SASE combines wide-area connectivity with security functions including firewall as a service, secure web gateway, cloud access security broker, and zero trust network access, all delivered from cloud points of presence close to the user. It suits remote and mobile workforces because protection follows the user instead of forcing traffic back to a data center. On the exam, 'provide consistent security to remote users and branches without backhauling' or 'converged network and security as a cloud service' means SASE."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"netsec",q:"Network access control (NAC) with posture assessment",a:"#Admit devices only after checking identity and health\nNon-compliant hosts go to a quarantine or remediation VLAN",s:"Health-check devices before granting network access",
  x:"NAC extends 802.1X style admission control with a posture assessment: an agent or agentless scan verifies patch level, antivirus status, and configuration before the switch or wireless controller assigns the device to a production VLAN. Failing devices are placed in a restricted network where they can update and retry. On the exam, 'ensure devices meet security requirements before connecting' or 'quarantine VLAN for out-of-date hosts' points to NAC; plain 802.1X only checks credentials, not health."},
 {obj:"1.2",c:"netsec",q:"Zero-trust policy enforcement point",a:"#Data-plane gate that allows or denies each access request\nActs on decisions from the policy engine and administrator in the control plane",s:"Data-plane gate executing the policy engine's decision",v:"zerotrust",
  x:"In the zero trust model described by NIST 800-207 and SY0-701, the control plane contains the policy engine, which evaluates identity, device posture, and context against policy, and the policy administrator, which issues the decision. The policy enforcement point sits in the data plane and actually opens or closes the connection to a resource, treating the subject as an implicit trust zone only after approval. The exam asks which component enforces access (PEP) versus decides it (policy engine)."},

 /* ---------- data (22) ---------- */
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Regulated data",a:"#Data whose handling is dictated by law or regulation\nPII, PHI, and cardholder data under GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS",s:"Data with legally mandated handling requirements",
  x:"Regulated data is any information for which an external law, regulation, or industry standard prescribes how it must be collected, stored, protected, and disclosed, such as personal data under GDPR, health records under HIPAA, or card numbers under PCI DSS. The organization does not get to choose the control level; the regulator does, and breaches trigger notification and fines. On the exam, a scenario naming a specific statute or 'personal health information' is asking about regulated data and its mandatory protections."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Trade secret vs intellectual property vs legal and financial data",a:"#Trade secret: value comes from secrecy; IP: legally registered rights; legal/financial: privileged or audit-sensitive",s:"Secret formulas vs registered rights vs privileged records",
  x:"SY0-701 lists several data types beyond regulated data. A trade secret, like a recipe or algorithm, is valuable only while confidential and has no protection once leaked. Intellectual property covers patents, copyrights, and trademarks that are legally registered and enforceable even if public. Legal information includes contracts and privileged communications, and financial information covers statements, payroll, and transactions subject to audit. The exam asks what type a formula, a patent, or attorney-client mail represents."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Public classification",a:"#No harm if disclosed\nMarketing material, published reports, and anything already released",s:"Disclosure causes no harm",g:"dataclass",
  x:"Public is the lowest classification level: information that is intended for release or would cause no damage if it were disclosed, such as press releases, product brochures, and published policies. It still needs integrity protection so it cannot be tampered with, but confidentiality controls are unnecessary. On the exam, 'can be freely shared with anyone' or 'already available on the website' means public; in government schemes the equivalent lowest tier is called unclassified."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Private classification",a:"#Personal or internal information not for outside release\nEmployee records and internal memos",s:"Internal or personal data kept inside the organization",g:"dataclass",
  x:"Private (often equated with internal use only) covers information about individuals or internal operations that should stay within the organization but whose exposure would cause limited harm, such as staff directories, internal procedures, and personnel files. In some schemes private specifically refers to personal data about employees or customers. The exam signals it with 'internal use only' or 'personal information of employees'; it sits above public and below confidential in most classification ladders."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Sensitive classification",a:"#Disclosure would cause harm to the organization or individuals\nA general label for data needing more than baseline protection",s:"Exposure would cause harm; above internal-use level",g:"dataclass",
  x:"Sensitive is used both as an umbrella term for anything above public and as a specific tier for information whose disclosure would damage the organization or people it describes, for example intellectual property, security configurations, or customer PII. It demands access control, encryption, and handling procedures beyond internal-only data. On the exam, 'would cause harm if disclosed' or 'requires special handling' points to sensitive; the exact ordering of tiers varies by organization, so read the scenario's own scheme."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Confidential classification",a:"#Highly restricted; disclosure causes serious damage\nTrade secrets, merger plans, and source code",s:"Serious damage if disclosed; tightly need-to-know",g:"dataclass",
  x:"Confidential information is limited to specific individuals with a strict need to know because exposure would cause serious financial, legal, or competitive damage. Examples include merger negotiations, trade secrets, and unreleased financial results. Controls typically include encryption at rest and in transit, strong authentication, DLP monitoring, and explicit access approvals. On the exam, 'only executives and legal counsel may access' or 'serious harm to the company' signals confidential rather than private or sensitive."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Restricted classification",a:"#Most limited access, often by regulation or contract\nHandling and distribution explicitly controlled",s:"Highest tier with explicit handling and distribution limits",g:"dataclass",
  x:"Restricted is commonly the top of the commercial classification ladder: data whose access is limited to named roles and whose copying, printing, and transmission are explicitly governed, often because a regulation, contract, or export control requires it. Payment card data, cryptographic keys, and classified government inputs fall here. The exam cue is 'access limited by law or contract' or 'must not leave the secure facility'; it implies the strongest technical and procedural controls."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Critical classification",a:"#Data essential to operations; loss would halt the business\nDrives availability priority, not just confidentiality",s:"Loss or corruption would stop core operations",g:"dataclass",
  x:"Critical describes information the organization cannot function without, such as transaction ledgers, patient records during care, or control system configurations. Unlike the other tiers, which primarily rank confidentiality, critical is about availability and integrity: it must be backed up, replicated, and recoverable within the RTO. On the exam, 'the business could not operate if this data were unavailable' signals critical, and the follow-up controls are redundancy and recovery rather than access restriction alone."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Data at rest",a:"#Stored on disk, tape, or in a database\nProtected by full-disk, file, or database encryption",s:"Stored data protected by disk or database encryption",g:"datastate",
  x:"Data at rest is information that is persisted on storage media and not currently moving or being processed. Its main threats are theft of the device or media and unauthorized access to the file system, so the standard protection is encryption (full-disk, file-level, or database column/TDE) plus access controls and backups. On the exam, 'laptop was stolen', 'database on the server', or 'backup tapes' indicates at rest, and the answer is nearly always encryption of the storage.",
  w:"At rest: sitting on the shelf, lock the shelf."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Data in transit",a:"#Moving across a network\nProtected by TLS, IPsec, or other transport encryption",s:"Data crossing a network protected by TLS or IPsec",g:"datastate",
  x:"Data in transit (in motion) is information traveling between systems, whether over a LAN, the internet, or a wireless link. The threats are interception and modification by an on-path attacker, so protections are transport encryption such as TLS, IPsec VPNs, SSH, and secure email, plus integrity checks. On the exam, 'sniffed on the network', 'sent over a public Wi-Fi', or 'between the client and the server' signals in transit, and the answer names an encrypted protocol.",
  w:"In transit: on the road, armored truck."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Data in use",a:"#Being processed in memory or CPU\nHardest state to protect; secure enclaves and memory encryption help",s:"Data being processed in RAM, hardest state to secure",g:"datastate",
  x:"Data in use is information loaded into memory and actively being processed by an application, where it must be decrypted to be worked on, making it vulnerable to memory scraping, malware, and privileged insiders. Protections include secure enclaves and trusted execution environments, memory encryption, application-level access controls, and endpoint hardening. On the exam, 'in memory', 'while the application is processing', or 'RAM' points to in use; homomorphic encryption is the emerging answer to computing on encrypted data.",
  w:"In use: on the workbench, keep the room secure."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Data sovereignty and geographic restrictions",a:"#Data is subject to the laws of the country where it is stored\nPin storage to approved regions and geofence access by location",s:"Stored data falls under the law of its host country",
  x:"Data sovereignty is the principle that data is governed by the legal system of the nation in which it physically resides, regardless of who owns it, so choosing a cloud region can subject data to foreign access laws or break rules that keep citizens' data in-country. Geographic restrictions are the matching controls: keeping replicas in approved regions and geofencing logins by country, remembering IP-based location can be spoofed by VPN. The exam cue is 'must remain within national borders' or 'restrict access based on country of origin'."},
 {obj:"3.3",c:"data",q:"Masking vs tokenization vs obfuscation",a:"#Masking hides part of a value; tokenization replaces it with a reversible stand-in; obfuscation makes it hard to read\nAll protect data without full encryption",s:"Partial hiding vs vault-backed token vs hard to read",
  x:"Data masking shows only part of a value, such as the last four card digits, and is irreversible in that view. Tokenization swaps the sensitive value for a random token and stores the mapping in a secure vault, so the original can be retrieved by authorized systems, which is why payment processors use it. Obfuscation is merely making data or code harder to read, not a secure control. The exam asks which lets a call center see part of a number (masking) or which removes the real value from the database while keeping its format (tokenization)."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"data",q:"Data loss prevention (DLP)",a:"#Detects and blocks sensitive data leaving approved boundaries\nInspects email, web uploads, USB, and cloud storage for labeled content",s:"Blocks classified data from leaving via email, web, or USB",
  x:"DLP systems inspect content in motion, at rest, and in use for patterns such as card numbers, classification labels, or fingerprints of protected documents, then block, quarantine, or encrypt the transfer according to policy. They run on endpoints, mail gateways, web proxies, and cloud services. On the exam, 'prevent employees from emailing customer records' or 'block copying confidential files to removable media' signals DLP; it depends on accurate data classification to work."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"High availability — load balancing vs clustering",a:"#Load balancing shares requests across independent servers; clustering makes nodes act as one system with shared state\nBoth remove single points of failure",s:"Distribute requests vs nodes coordinating as one system",
  x:"Load balancing places a device in front of several stateless servers and spreads connections among them, so the loss of one node simply removes it from rotation. Clustering links servers so they coordinate, share storage or state, and fail over transparently, which suits databases and file services where every node must see the same data. The exam distinguishes them with 'stateless web tier' (load balancing) versus 'database must fail over without data loss' (cluster), and active/active versus active/passive describes how the members share work."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Hot site",a:"#Fully equipped duplicate ready to run within minutes\nLive systems and current data; highest cost",s:"Fully operational duplicate, near-instant failover",g:"site",
  x:"A hot site is an alternate facility with hardware, software, network connectivity, and near-real-time replicated data already in place, so operations can shift to it almost immediately. It supports the shortest RTO and RPO but is the most expensive option because it duplicates production continuously. On the exam, 'must resume operations within minutes' or 'data replicated continuously to the alternate site' signals a hot site; contrast with warm (hours to days) and cold (days to weeks).",
  w:"Hot: lights already on, chairs still warm."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Warm site",a:"#Equipped facility that needs data restored and systems brought up\nHours to a day or two; middle cost",s:"Hardware ready but data must be loaded; hours to recover",g:"site",
  x:"A warm site has power, connectivity, and typically the servers and network gear installed, but the data is not current; recent backups must be restored and systems configured before it can take over. It balances cost against recovery time and is the most common compromise. The exam cue is 'equipment in place but needs the latest backups loaded' or an RTO measured in hours to days; it is faster than cold and cheaper than hot."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Cold site",a:"#Empty facility with power and space only\nEquipment must be procured and installed; days or weeks to recover",s:"Bare facility, equipment must be brought in; slowest",g:"site",
  x:"A cold site is little more than a leased space with power, cooling, and possibly network cabling; no servers or data are present, so recovery means shipping in equipment, building systems, and restoring backups. It is the cheapest alternate site but has the longest recovery time, suiting organizations that can tolerate extended outages. On the exam, 'lowest cost' or 'recovery could take weeks' identifies a cold site.",
  w:"Cold: an empty room and a promise."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Geographic dispersion, platform diversity, and multi-cloud",a:"#Spread sites, vendors, and technologies so one event cannot take everything down\nDistance defeats disasters; diversity defeats a single flaw",s:"Spread sites, vendors, platforms to avoid shared failure",
  x:"Resilience planning uses geographic dispersion so that a regional disaster such as an earthquake or grid failure cannot hit both primary and backup sites; platform diversity so that one vendor's vulnerability or outage does not affect every system; and multi-cloud so workloads are not dependent on a single provider. The trade-off is complexity and cost. On the exam, 'both data centers are in the same city' is the flaw, and 'avoid vendor lock-in' or 'a single zero-day affecting all devices' points to diversity."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Continuity of operations plan (COOP) and capacity planning",a:"#COOP keeps essential functions running during and after a disruption\nCapacity planning ensures people, technology, and infrastructure can absorb the load",s:"Sustain essential functions; plan staff and tech capacity",
  x:"A continuity of operations plan defines how mission-essential functions continue through an incident, including alternate sites, delegated authority, and manual workarounds. Capacity planning is the SY0-701 term for ensuring enough trained staff, technology, and infrastructure headroom to handle growth and failover, so a backup site can actually carry the workload. On the exam, 'continue critical operations' signals COOP and 'ensure the recovery site can handle the full production load' signals capacity planning."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Tabletop exercise",a:"#Discussion-based walkthrough of a scenario\nNo systems touched; validates the plan and roles",s:"Talk-through of a scenario with no live systems",g:"drtest",
  x:"A tabletop exercise gathers the response and recovery team around a table to step through a hypothetical disaster or incident, discussing what each person would do, which procedures apply, and where the plan has gaps. It is inexpensive and low risk but proves nothing about whether the technology actually works. On the exam, 'discussion-based', 'no impact on production', or 'validate roles and communications' identifies a tabletop; it is usually the first level of testing."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Failover test",a:"#Deliberately switch production to the backup system or site\nProves the redundancy actually works",s:"Live switch to the backup to prove it works",g:"drtest",
  x:"A failover test intentionally takes the primary system, cluster node, or site offline and confirms that services move to the redundant component within the expected time. It is the most realistic validation of high availability and site readiness but carries risk to production if the failover does not work as designed. The exam cue is 'verify the secondary site can take over' or 'switch operations to the backup system'; it is more disruptive than a simulation or parallel test."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Simulation exercise",a:"#Scripted scenario executed with responders acting in real time\nMore realistic than tabletop, typically without touching production",s:"Real-time scripted drill without production impact",g:"drtest",
  x:"A simulation runs a realistic scenario, such as a mock ransomware outbreak or a fire drill, with participants performing their actual response steps, possibly in a test environment, under time pressure. It tests coordination and decision-making more rigorously than a tabletop while avoiding the risk of a full failover. On the exam, 'realistic drill' or 'responders perform actions in a test environment' indicates a simulation, sitting between tabletop (talk only) and failover or parallel (real systems)."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Parallel processing test",a:"#Run the recovery site alongside production and compare results\nValidates the backup without cutting production over",s:"Recovery site runs alongside production for comparison",g:"drtest",
  x:"In a parallel processing test the alternate site or backup systems are brought up and process the same workload as production, and outputs are compared to prove the recovery environment is complete and accurate. Production is never interrupted, which makes it safer than a failover test yet far more thorough than a tabletop. On the exam, 'both sites process transactions at the same time' or 'verify the backup site without disrupting operations' signals parallel processing."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Full backup",a:"#Copies everything every time\nFastest restore, slowest and largest to create",s:"Every file copied each time; one set to restore",g:"backup",v:"backups",
  x:"A full backup captures all selected data regardless of when it last changed, so a single backup set is enough to restore completely. The cost is time and storage, which is why organizations usually take a full backup weekly and supplement it with incremental or differential backups. On the exam, 'fastest restore' or 'requires only one backup set' indicates full; it also resets the archive bit on Windows systems, which is how the other types know what changed."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Incremental backup",a:"#Copies only changes since the last backup of any type\nSmallest and fastest to take; restore needs full plus every incremental",s:"Changes since last backup; restore needs every increment",g:"backup",v:"backups",
  x:"An incremental backup saves only files modified since the most recent backup, whether that was full or incremental, and clears the archive bit. Each run is small and fast, but a restore requires the last full backup followed by every incremental in order, so a single missing tape breaks the chain. The exam cue is 'minimize backup time and storage' for the benefit and 'longest restore' for the drawback.",
  w:"Incremental = many small steps, and you must retrace every step."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Differential backup",a:"#Copies everything changed since the last full backup\nGrows each day; restore needs full plus the latest differential only",s:"Changes since last full; restore needs full plus latest",g:"backup",v:"backups",
  x:"A differential backup captures all files changed since the last full backup and does not clear the archive bit, so each differential is larger than the one before until the next full resets it. Restoring needs just two sets, the full and the most recent differential, which is faster and less fragile than an incremental chain. On the exam, 'restore using the full backup and one other backup' identifies differential; 'backup size increases daily' is its trade-off.",
  w:"Differential = the difference from the last full, one bigger snapshot each day."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Snapshots, replication, and journaling",a:"#Snapshot: point-in-time image; replication: live copy elsewhere; journaling: log of changes for replay\nComplement, not replace, backups",s:"Point-in-time image vs live copy vs change log",
  x:"A snapshot freezes the state of a volume or VM at an instant so it can be reverted quickly, but it lives on the same storage and is not a true backup. Replication continuously copies data to another system or site for availability, which also copies corruption and deletions unless it is delayed. Journaling records every change in a log so data can be replayed to any point in time, enabling very small RPOs. The exam asks which offers near-zero RPO (replication or journaling), which allows instant rollback (snapshot), and why none replaces offline backups."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"Onsite vs offsite backups and backup encryption",a:"#Keep a copy away from the primary site and encrypt every copy\nOffsite survives the disaster that destroys the building",s:"Store copies off-premises and encrypt them",
  x:"Onsite backups restore fastest but are lost in a fire, flood, or ransomware event that hits the whole facility; offsite copies, whether tapes in a vault or cloud storage, survive site-level disasters at the cost of slower retrieval. Because backup media travels and sits unattended, it must be encrypted so a lost tape or exposed bucket does not become a breach. The exam cue is 'protect backups from a disaster at the primary site' (offsite) and 'lost backup tapes contained customer data' (encryption); the 3-2-1 rule captures the practice."},
 {obj:"3.4",c:"data",q:"UPS vs generator",a:"#UPS bridges seconds to minutes on battery; generator runs for hours or days on fuel\nUPS covers the gap until the generator starts",s:"Short battery bridge vs long-duration fuel-powered supply",
  x:"An uninterruptible power supply uses batteries to keep equipment running instantly when utility power fails, providing enough time for a clean shutdown or for a generator to spin up, and it also conditions power against surges and sags. A generator takes seconds to start but can supply a site for as long as fuel lasts. On the exam, 'ride through a brief outage' or 'allow graceful shutdown' is UPS; 'sustain operations during an extended outage' is generator; dual power feeds and PDUs round out power resilience."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"data",q:"RTO — Recovery Time Objective",a:"#Maximum acceptable time to restore a service after disruption\nDrives choice of hot, warm, or cold recovery",s:"Max tolerable downtime before service is restored",g:"bia",
  x:"The recovery time objective is the target duration within which a business process or system must be back in operation after an outage before unacceptable consequences occur. A short RTO justifies expensive options such as hot sites and clustering; a long one permits cold sites and manual rebuilds. On the exam, 'must be restored within four hours' is an RTO statement, and it is contrasted with RPO, which is about data loss rather than downtime."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"data",q:"RPO — Recovery Point Objective",a:"#Maximum acceptable data loss measured in time\nSets how often backups or replication must run",s:"Max tolerable data loss, expressed as time since last copy",g:"bia",
  x:"The recovery point objective defines how far back in time the restored data may be relative to the moment of failure, which translates directly into backup frequency: an RPO of one hour requires backups or replication at least hourly. It is set by the business based on how much rework or lost transactions are tolerable. On the exam, 'can afford to lose no more than 15 minutes of data' is RPO, whereas 'must be running again within' is RTO.",
  w:"RPO = how much data you can afford to lose; RTO = how long you can be down."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"data",q:"MTTR — Mean Time to Repair",a:"#Average time to fix a failed component and return it to service\nLower MTTR means faster recovery",s:"Average time to restore a failed component",g:"bia",
  x:"Mean time to repair (or recover) is the average elapsed time from a failure until the system is operational again, including diagnosis, replacement, and testing. It measures maintainability and the effectiveness of the support process, and it must be shorter than the RTO for the plan to be achievable. On the exam, 'average time technicians take to restore the server' signals MTTR; contrast with MTBF, which measures how often failures happen, not how long they take to fix."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"data",q:"MTBF — Mean Time Between Failures",a:"#Average operating time between one failure and the next\nA reliability measure used to plan spares and replacements",s:"Average uptime between failures; reliability metric",g:"bia",
  x:"Mean time between failures is the expected interval between failures for a repairable component, typically supplied by the manufacturer from testing. A higher MTBF means a more reliable device and informs how many spares to stock and when to replace equipment proactively. The exam pairs it with MTTR: MTBF describes how frequently something breaks, MTTR how quickly it is fixed, and availability is roughly MTBF divided by MTBF plus MTTR. For non-repairable items the analogous term is MTTF."},

 /* ---------- harden (16) ---------- */
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Secure baseline — establish, deploy, maintain",a:"#Define the hardened configuration, roll it out consistently, keep it current\nBaselines such as CIS Benchmarks are the reference",s:"Define, roll out, and keep current a standard secure config",
  x:"A secure baseline is the documented set of settings a system type must have, such as disabled services, password policy, and logging levels, often derived from CIS Benchmarks or vendor guides. SY0-701 splits the work into establishing the baseline, deploying it through images, group policy, or configuration management, and maintaining it by monitoring for drift and updating as threats and patches change. The exam cue is 'ensure all servers are configured consistently' (deploy) or 'systems have drifted from the standard' (maintain)."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Hardening targets — mobile devices and workstations",a:"#Encrypt storage, enforce screen lock and MDM, patch OS and apps\nRemove local admin and unneeded software on workstations",s:"Encryption, lock, MDM, patching, no local admin",
  x:"Hardening a workstation means applying the baseline: full-disk encryption, host firewall and EDR, automatic patching, removal of unnecessary applications, and standard user accounts without local administrator rights. Mobile devices add MDM enrollment, mandatory passcodes, remote wipe, and restrictions on side-loading and jailbreaking. On the exam, 'reduce the attack surface of user endpoints' or 'protect data on a lost laptop or phone' invokes these controls, with encryption and remote wipe as the recurring answers."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Hardening targets — switches and routers",a:"#Change default credentials, disable unused ports and services, use SSH and SNMPv3\nRestrict management to a dedicated VLAN or jump server",s:"No defaults, unused ports off, SSH/SNMPv3, mgmt VLAN",
  x:"Network infrastructure hardening starts with replacing default passwords and disabling unused physical ports, insecure services like Telnet and HTTP management, and legacy protocols. Management should use SSH and SNMPv3 from a restricted management network, with ACLs limiting who can reach the device, plus port security, DHCP snooping, and disabling unused switch ports. On the exam, 'secure the management interface of network devices' points here, with Telnet or SNMPv1 in the scenario being the obvious flaw."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Hardening targets — servers and cloud infrastructure",a:"#Minimal roles and services, patch schedule, least-privilege IAM, logging on\nCloud adds locked-down storage permissions and key management",s:"Minimal services, patches, least-privilege IAM, logging",
  x:"Server hardening removes every role, service, and account not required for the server's function, applies vendor patches on a defined schedule, enforces host firewall rules, and ships logs to a central collector. Cloud infrastructure extends the same idea to configuration: private storage buckets, least-privilege IAM policies, disabled public IPs where unnecessary, encryption keys under customer control, and IaC templates scanned before deployment. The exam contrasts 'the provider secures the physical hosts' with 'the customer hardens what they deploy'."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Hardening targets — ICS/SCADA, embedded, RTOS, and IoT",a:"#Isolate on separate networks, change defaults, control firmware updates\nCompensate for devices that cannot run agents or be patched",s:"Isolation and firmware control for unpatchable devices",
  x:"Devices that cannot take a standard baseline, including PLCs, medical devices, RTOS controllers, and consumer IoT, are hardened by what surrounds them: dedicated VLANs or air gaps, firewalls that allow only required protocols, disabling unused interfaces, changing default credentials, and managing firmware through vendor-approved channels. Monitoring watches for anomalies since on-device prevention is limited. On the exam, 'cannot install security software' points to these compensating steps."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Wireless site survey and heat map",a:"#Measure signal strength and interference across the space; map it visually\nPlaces APs for coverage without leaking outside the building",s:"Measure and map Wi-Fi coverage to place access points",
  x:"A site survey walks the facility with tools that record signal strength, channel usage, and interference sources, and a heat map renders those measurements as a color-coded floor plan. Together they determine where access points go, what channels and power levels to use, and where coverage bleeds into parking lots or neighboring suites where an attacker could connect. On the exam, 'determine optimal AP placement' or 'identify dead zones and overlap' signals a site survey and heat map."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Mobile device management (MDM)",a:"#Central console to enforce policy on phones and tablets\nPasscodes, encryption, app control, remote lock and wipe",s:"Central policy enforcement and remote wipe for mobiles",
  x:"MDM (often part of a broader unified endpoint management suite) enrolls devices and then pushes configuration: required passcodes, storage encryption, allowed and blocked applications, VPN and Wi-Fi profiles, geofencing, and detection of rooted or jailbroken devices. It can remotely lock or wipe a device, and on personal devices can wipe only the corporate container. The exam cue is 'enforce security settings on employee smartphones' or 'wipe a lost device'."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"BYOD — Bring Your Own Device",a:"#Employee owns the device and uses it for work\nLeast control; containerization and MDM limit corporate data exposure",s:"Personal device used for work; least corporate control",g:"mobiledeploy",
  x:"Under a BYOD model employees use their personal phones or laptops for company access, which saves hardware cost and pleases users but gives the organization the least control over patching, apps, and data separation. Mitigations are MDM enrollment with a corporate work profile or container, conditional access, and an acceptable use agreement covering wipe rights. On the exam, 'employees use their own phones' with concerns about privacy and data separation identifies BYOD."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"COPE — Corporate-Owned, Personally Enabled",a:"#Company owns the device but allows personal use\nFull corporate control with some user flexibility",s:"Company-owned device, personal use permitted",g:"mobiledeploy",
  x:"COPE devices are purchased and managed by the organization, so they can be fully enrolled, imaged, and monitored, while employees are allowed a reasonable amount of personal use such as private apps and email. It provides the strongest security of the common models while keeping staff satisfied. On the exam, 'the company provides the phone and permits personal use' is COPE; contrast with BYOD (user-owned) and CYOD (user chooses from a company list).",
  w:"COPE: Company Owns, Personal use Enabled."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"CYOD — Choose Your Own Device",a:"#Employee picks from a company-approved list; company owns and manages it\nStandardized support with some user choice",s:"User picks from an approved list; company owns it",g:"mobiledeploy",
  x:"CYOD offers employees a catalog of pre-approved devices, and the organization purchases, provisions, and manages whichever one is chosen. It limits the support burden to known models while giving users a say, and it keeps full corporate control over configuration. The exam distinguishes it from COPE mainly by the element of user selection and from BYOD by ownership; 'select from an approved list of devices' is the identifying phrase."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"WPA3 and SAE",a:"#WPA3-Personal replaces the PSK handshake with Simultaneous Authentication of Equals\nResists offline dictionary attacks and provides forward secrecy",s:"SAE handshake defeats offline PSK cracking",
  x:"WPA3 is the current Wi-Fi security standard. In personal mode it uses SAE, a Diffie-Hellman based password-authenticated key exchange (the Dragonfly handshake), so a captured handshake cannot be brute-forced offline the way WPA2's four-way PSK exchange can, and each session gets a unique key for forward secrecy. WPA3 also adds protected management frames and, in enterprise mode, a 192-bit security suite. On the exam, 'prevent offline cracking of the Wi-Fi passphrase' or 'SAE' points to WPA3.",
  w:"SAE: both sides prove the password without ever sending it."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Enterprise Wi-Fi with RADIUS and EAP",a:"#WPA3/WPA2-Enterprise authenticates each user through a RADIUS server\nPer-user credentials or certificates instead of a shared passphrase",s:"Per-user RADIUS authentication instead of a shared key",v:"dot1x",
  x:"Enterprise mode uses 802.1X so the access point forwards each client's EAP exchange to a RADIUS server, which checks the user's directory credentials or certificate and returns a unique session key. This eliminates the shared passphrase that must be rotated whenever someone leaves and enables per-user logging and dynamic VLAN assignment. On the exam, 'authenticate wireless users with their domain accounts' or 'avoid a single shared pre-shared key' signals WPA-Enterprise with RADIUS; RADIUS federation extends it across organizations."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Input validation",a:"#Accept only data that matches expected type, length, format, and range\nServer-side validation is the one that counts",s:"Reject input that fails type, length, format, range checks",
  x:"Input validation checks every value received from a user or another system against strict rules before it is used, ideally with an allow list of acceptable patterns rather than a block list of bad ones, and always on the server because client-side checks can be bypassed. It is the primary defense against injection attacks, buffer overflows, and cross-site scripting. On the exam, 'prevent SQL injection and XSS at the source' or 'sanitize user input' points to input validation, often paired with output encoding and parameterized queries."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Secure cookies",a:"#Cookies flagged Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite\nSent only over HTTPS, hidden from scripts, and not with cross-site requests",s:"Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite flags on session cookies",
  x:"A session cookie carries the user's authenticated state, so its attributes matter. The Secure flag prevents the browser sending it over unencrypted HTTP, HttpOnly blocks JavaScript from reading it so XSS cannot steal the session, and SameSite limits it being included in cross-site requests, which blunts CSRF. On the exam, 'protect the session token from being captured' or 'cookie transmitted over HTTP' points to these flags, along with short expiration and regenerating the ID after login."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Static code analysis vs code signing",a:"#Static analysis reviews source for flaws before running; code signing proves who published the code and that it is unaltered\nOne finds bugs, the other proves authenticity",s:"Scan source for bugs vs sign binary to prove publisher",
  x:"Static application security testing tools parse source or bytecode without executing it to flag insecure patterns such as unchecked input, hardcoded secrets, and dangerous functions; dynamic analysis instead tests the running app. Code signing applies the developer's private key to a hash of the finished binary so users and systems can verify the publisher and detect tampering. On the exam, 'review the code before compilation' is static analysis; 'verify the application came from the vendor and was not modified' is code signing."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Sandboxing and monitoring as application-security controls",a:"#Sandbox runs untrusted code in an isolated environment; monitoring watches behavior in production\nContain first, then observe",s:"Isolated execution of untrusted code plus runtime watch",
  x:"Sandboxing executes an application or suspicious file in a confined environment, whether a VM, container, or browser process, with no access to the real system, so malicious behavior can be detected without harm; it is used by analysts, email gateways, and mobile operating systems alike. Monitoring covers application logs, performance baselines, and runtime protections that detect anomalous behavior after deployment. On the exam, 'test a suspicious attachment safely' means sandbox, and 'detect unusual application behavior after release' means monitoring."},
];
